WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.520
Welcome to The Test Set. Here we talk with some of the brightest thinkers and tinkerers

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.440
in statistical analysis, scientific computing, and machine learning. Digging into what makes

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:18.480
them tick, plus the insights, experiments, and OMG moments that shape the field.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.160
In this episode we talk with Trevor Manz. Trevor started out in chemistry and computational

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.760
biology, before moving into visualization research, where he ran into a problem. Researchers

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.639
could build these sophisticated tools, but getting them into the hands of people analyzing

00:00:32.639 --> 00:00:38.119
data was another story. That frustration eventually led him towards Python notebooks, and to creating

00:00:38.119 --> 00:00:43.240
AnyWidget, which makes it easier to build interactive tools that work across the computational

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.480
notebook ecosystem. Today he's a founding engineer at Marimo, where he's pushing that

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.840
idea in a new direction. If notebooks are such a useful place for humans to explore

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:58.000
data, could they also be a better place for AI agents to do it? And what would AI agents

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:04.599
need to use notebooks effectively? We're joined by self-proclaimed notebook skeptic

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:10.040
Hadley Wickham, which I think makes for a fun discussion. So with that, Trevor Manz.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:18.400
Trevor, welcome to The Test Set. So you're Trevor Manz, scientist, programmer, and as

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:24.279
you say, Wisconsinite, which I'm excited to dig into. You created AnyWidget and you're

00:01:24.279 --> 00:01:29.879
a founding engineer at Marimo. Yeah. Thanks so much for coming on. So happy to have you.

00:01:29.879 --> 00:01:32.680
Yeah. Thanks so much for having me.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:38.199
And by way of introduction, I guess I'm, I'm Michael Chow, the host of The Test Set, and

00:01:38.199 --> 00:01:44.400
this is Hadley Wickham, co-host and chief scientist at Fawcett. Yeah, sure. I'm so excited.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.279
I know you've been doing a lot with Marimo and notebooks, and especially with Marimo

00:01:50.279 --> 00:01:57.800
pair. I'm, I think, yeah, I'm so curious to talk a bit more about Marimo and notebooks.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:03.680
Maybe just to kick it off, you could explain a little bit about what Marimo is and kind

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.279
of like who uses it and what, what excites you about it?

00:02:07.279 --> 00:02:11.279
Yeah. So Marimo, Marimo is like a new kind of...

00:02:11.279 --> 00:02:13.279
Marimo, I'm so sorry. Yeah.

00:02:14.279 --> 00:02:21.360
So Marimo is a new kind of Python notebook and I like to describe them as a delightful

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.639
reactive Python notebook. And the first piece is that we're a delightful notebook insofar

00:02:27.639 --> 00:02:32.039
as that we've been able to look a lot at how notebooks are used in practice and then really

00:02:32.039 --> 00:02:38.080
think about creating a very built-in user experience for many different ways that notebooks

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.639
are used, like traditional notebooks are being used. And then the second piece there is that

00:02:42.639 --> 00:02:49.440
we are a reactive notebook. So similar to observable notebooks or Pluto, which are sort

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.479
of from the Julia ecosystem, Marimo does not... Marimo understands the relationships between

00:02:55.479 --> 00:02:59.360
your cells, not based off of the ordering that you wrote the cells, but the actual data

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:03.839
flow. So where your variables are declared and how they're used to find the execution

00:03:03.839 --> 00:03:09.720
order of your notebook. And that leads to eliminating this kind of hidden state that

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:16.399
can come from having sort of a traditional Python REPL or like a traditional Python notebook.

00:03:16.399 --> 00:03:19.320
And then the third piece is that we are a Python notebook. So we're like only focused

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:24.440
on Python. And that is all the way from a lot of the user interfaces oriented around

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:29.160
data structures that are in the Python ecosystem. We have things like a built-in package manager.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.360
So for when you're trying to work on your data, we can sell packages for you. All the

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.639
way down to the file format itself is not like a JSON file. It's actually a Python script.

00:03:37.639 --> 00:03:42.960
So yeah, those are the three things I think that sort of characterize Marimo in contrast

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.000
to other sort of computational notebooks.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.440
Yeah. So, and I appreciate you helping with the pronunciation. I feel like this also came

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.000
up at PyCon when I saw y'all that this is the perfect chance that I'm going to work

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:01.199
Marimo in and Marimo out forever. So I'm glad that we have this to practice.

00:04:01.199 --> 00:04:04.119
But happy to help out there.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:09.679
It's interesting to hear too, you mentioned like as a package manager and some of that,

00:04:09.679 --> 00:04:15.800
like maybe the surprising scope of things that kind of enter in to a notebook. Like

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:21.640
if you want to be delightful and useful, that even it sounds like package management kind

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.920
of becomes an important piece.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:29.200
So we like to say that Marimo is like, you know, we're an open source computational notebook

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:35.799
that's used across like industry research and education. And then beyond that, I think

00:04:35.799 --> 00:04:39.959
as well from being able to look at the different ways that people are trying to use traditional

00:04:39.959 --> 00:04:43.920
notebooks, we've also thought a lot about, you know, you build up a notebook, but like

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.320
what are the directions that you might want to take it? So some notebooks look more like

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.600
doing like data pipelines and those you can run as Python scripts and maybe have very

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:57.000
few visual elements. There's some that maybe are more in this, you know, world of like

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.239
literate programming where it's both like your code, your code is very forward. And

00:05:00.239 --> 00:05:05.000
then you have pros explaining and contextualizing the code. And then we have, you know, because

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:12.000
we're a reactive Python notebook, the cells can react to user interactions. And so there's

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.320
a mode within Marimo that we call like app mode that you can just hide all of your code

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.440
and then you just have the outputs. And so for that set of users, it's more like presentational

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.920
or, you know, showing people almost these like data apps, but without having to show

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.959
the code. And I think that's, so it's quite hard to like generalize like what that user

00:05:28.959 --> 00:05:34.200
group is. But I think if I could generalize it, it would be, you know, individuals that

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:39.019
are not professional software engineers, but like have traditionally learned a little bit

00:05:39.019 --> 00:05:42.720
of code to do some thing with data.

