And do you think, is it like mostly like data people broadly construed or are there people

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.880
doing like non-data stuff? I'm sure there are people doing non-data stuff, but is that

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:56.679
like a user base you think about?

00:05:56.679 --> 00:06:03.399
Yeah, I guess one thing I've noticed by introducing like a reactive experience to an ecosystem

00:06:03.399 --> 00:06:10.079
like Python, we do have users that are, you know, trying to build, like they've sort of

00:06:10.079 --> 00:06:14.880
maybe learned a little bit of Python in their education and now they have sort of a toolkit

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.220
or that background with that coding language and now a toolkit that allows them to make

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:23.559
like programs a bit more interactive. So we do see things like games, I guess, being built

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.359
inside of Marimo. But I'd say in terms of like the scope of where we are taking the

00:06:28.359 --> 00:06:32.519
project, it's definitely oriented around, you know, generally folks have some unstructured

00:06:32.519 --> 00:06:37.200
data, they're going to like load it into memory and like, yeah, do something with that

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:42.320
data. Either that be, you know, perform some kind of analysis or maybe do some sort of

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:46.320
like presentational scaffold into like an application.

00:06:46.799 --> 00:06:53.640
I guess like more, sorry, more concrete, just to remind me of like, do you know processing?

00:06:54.359 --> 00:06:59.640
It was like an art, really like a framework for doing kind of art explorations. And it

