competitively. So I was like definitely in like the natural sciences, but did not

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:45.880
really work with computers that much.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:46.959
I mean, I use computers.

00:17:46.959 --> 00:17:50.280
I made websites for fun, but I didn't for my own like research.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:53.079
I was very much in a wet lab doing like organic synthesis.

00:17:53.079 --> 00:17:57.040
So it was it felt very distant from the type of work that I end up doing for my

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:58.040
research.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:02.000
Oh, is there a lot around it, like in Kenyon College?

00:18:02.359 --> 00:18:08.920
Yeah. So Kenyon College, it's in, it's about an hour outside of Columbus, Ohio.

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:11.319
So there are about 2,000 students.

00:18:12.719 --> 00:18:16.400
Yeah, being a liberal arts college, like I got a bachelor of arts in chemistry, so I

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:21.520
studied like humanities and things outside of like the natural sciences as well.

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:27.119
And I think it was just a really nice place to have faculty that were like very hands

00:18:27.119 --> 00:18:28.199
on with like learning.

00:18:28.199 --> 00:18:31.560
So like, you know, in contrast to going to some of the places where I ended up for

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:36.119
graduate school, like the priority at that university or at Kenyon College was like

00:18:37.079 --> 00:18:40.800
having faculty that were very like engaged and like trying to get folks into

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:46.520
research. So I had, I think it was my academic advisor had a lab and he was like,

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:49.040
hey, if you want to like do some research, you should you should try it out.

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:51.640
And I didn't, it sounded interesting.

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:54.359
So that's sort of where I started my research career, I guess.

00:18:54.439 --> 00:18:58.880
Yeah. And then from there you went to Cambridge, is that right?

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:03.119
Yeah, I tell you, it was like a bit of a journey, I guess, like because it was such a

00:19:03.119 --> 00:19:07.719
small school, I would like apply for various like summer research internships.

00:19:07.719 --> 00:19:12.760
And I ended up taking like an internship in St.

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:16.199
Louis, Missouri at the Danforth Plant Science Center.

00:19:16.199 --> 00:19:20.920
And I was doing a lot of like wet lab kind of like molecular biology stuff.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:23.119
But they ran this workshop with computer vision.

00:19:23.119 --> 00:19:26.439
And I was like the first time that I used like I think it was a Python program called

00:19:26.439 --> 00:19:28.880
like OpenCV. They gave us like a Raspberry Pi.

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:34.400
And I remember I was like that summer I was imaging a lot of like Arabidopsis, which

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:36.079
is this model organism.

00:19:36.079 --> 00:19:39.239
And I was measuring all the apertures by hand.

00:19:39.599 --> 00:19:44.079
And then like we ran this like computer vision program that like I saw like kind of

00:19:44.079 --> 00:19:45.239
automate a lot of that.

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:48.800
And I think it was like kind of this like revelation of like, oh, my gosh, I think I

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:50.760
should figure out how to use this stuff.

00:19:52.239 --> 00:19:57.000
But also it was like kind of interesting because we like it was a very visual like

00:19:57.359 --> 00:20:01.439
output, like you could kind of see where it measured things on the actual images and

00:20:01.439 --> 00:20:05.359
being able to like as a human, like look and observe that you could kind of see where

00:20:05.359 --> 00:20:09.479
the algorithms would like fail, being able to actually like highlight a region.

00:20:09.479 --> 00:20:13.920
And so I think that's I mean, in retrospect, it's easy to like construct some

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:17.680
narrative of when I started down this path into like visualization.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:21.640
But like that was probably somewhere we're having the somewhat like web background and

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:25.880
like also this like science background and seeing like how the ways that, you know,

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:32.760
visualizations can sort of surprise you or like confirm like some something that you

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:34.040
suspected in the data.

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:38.839
It became kind of this interest of mine to see like, yeah, if you know, if I am

00:20:38.839 --> 00:20:44.560
applying algorithms or using computers to like assist my under help myself automate

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:48.160
some of this stuff that was really tedious, like, are there ways that I can bring myself

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:51.359
in the loop to make sure I'm like pretty confident about like the application of

00:20:51.359 --> 00:20:52.199
those tools?

00:20:52.319 --> 00:20:55.359
Had you done a lot of computer programming up to that point?

00:20:56.280 --> 00:21:00.880
Not really. Yeah, I mostly like web, HTML, CSS, JavaScript kind of stuff.

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:06.800
And then after that summer, I think when I went back to doing more research, I got I

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:10.400
started like trying to use actually R was the first programming language I like really

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:12.520
sat down with to learn for data analysis.

00:21:12.719 --> 00:21:14.920
Never heard of it. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:21:15.359 --> 00:21:20.880
And and then I think I just got really interested in, yeah, like software generally

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:22.959
for like for doing data analysis.

00:21:22.959 --> 00:21:28.079
And that sort of led to, I guess, not really like a total pivot, but switching into like

00:21:28.479 --> 00:21:33.239
looking more into doing like computational research rather than like sitting under the

00:21:33.239 --> 00:21:36.079
hood with a pipette was sort of the direction that things went.

00:21:36.079 --> 00:21:40.560
And then from there, you went to Cambridge to do a master's.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:45.880
Were you, yeah, were you thinking of going like the academic route or kind of where

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:47.920
were you at that point?

00:21:49.439 --> 00:21:52.439
Yeah, I think at that point I was interested in doing.

00:21:53.719 --> 00:22:00.640
A PhD, maybe at some point, but I, I had, but because because of my background, like

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:04.640
I really wanted to spend a little bit of time, like doing some software stuff there,

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:10.920
like, like data or spending time improving my maths and sort of like becoming more of a

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:11.800
data person, maybe.

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:16.719
So that that was an interesting, yeah, like avenue, I guess it was sort of like a one

00:22:16.719 --> 00:22:17.439
year master's.

00:22:17.439 --> 00:22:23.319
And then after that, I joined into like a bioinformatics computational biology program

00:22:23.319 --> 00:22:24.199
for the PhD.

00:22:24.719 --> 00:22:26.040
How'd you like Cambridge?

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:29.400
Yeah, it was it was really interesting.

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:33.479
I mean, like coming from a very small liberal arts college and then also just being like

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:36.760
in the UK, like there are a lot of differences.

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:42.439
Also like a lot more European in terms of like the the population that was like at the

00:22:42.439 --> 00:22:43.760
school and also in the program.

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:47.640
So that was, I mean, probably the best part of that experience was making a, we had a

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:52.000
very small cohort and just making like very close friends that now are sprinkled kind

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:52.920
of like throughout Europe.

00:22:53.079 --> 00:22:59.040
But yeah, I think it was, it was a much different type of institution than like where I

00:22:59.040 --> 00:22:59.719
went to undergrad.

00:22:59.719 --> 00:23:04.000
So I think it also like taught me a lot about, you know, priorities in terms of research

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:08.520
or like like academic priorities for faculty at different universities.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:13.359
There's something interesting about, I feel like just all the places kind of you you

00:23:13.359 --> 00:23:13.880
bounce between.

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:19.560
It seems like a big cut of both like American Midwest and like the UK.

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:22.079
So it's interesting to hear about.

00:23:22.079 --> 00:23:26.719
I mean, I had to wear like a gown sometimes to like dinners at Cambridge, and that was

00:23:26.719 --> 00:23:31.319
very like, I remember not even like thinking I didn't need like a suit or something.

00:23:31.319 --> 00:23:34.959
And then I was really happy that I brought one because we oddly were wearing it a good

00:23:34.959 --> 00:23:35.319
amount.

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:40.920
So that actually reminded me, like my dad did his PhD at Cornell and he had like this

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:45.000
part-time job, like checking that people were wearing their jackets to dinner.

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:47.560
He was like jacket police.

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:48.000
Is that?

00:23:48.239 --> 00:23:48.640
Yeah.

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:49.959
Like the jacket bouncer.

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:50.359
Yeah.

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:59.760
And then I guess you mentioned, so you, you did your PhD at Harvard in the, what was the

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:00.760
lab called again?

00:24:01.439 --> 00:24:03.719
Yeah, it's called the, we're called the High Dive Group now.

00:24:03.719 --> 00:24:06.680
When I joined, the lead of the lab is Nils Gallenborg.

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:13.520
We rebranded at some point, I think to like, but we're like a visualization group.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:19.119
So we, the way that the, the research that we worked on where we have a lot of close

00:24:19.119 --> 00:24:24.359
collaborations with like experimental biologists in the department or outside of the

00:24:24.359 --> 00:24:25.400
department at Harvard.

00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:31.119
And then we kind of work with them to understand like their data problems and see if

00:24:31.119 --> 00:24:37.000
like visualization, like yeah, visualizations could help or like really connect folks to

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:37.599
their data.

00:24:37.959 --> 00:24:42.280
I think a nice thing with a field like biology is that a lot of the data are very

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:42.880
abstract.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:48.119
So there, and like new types of experiments come out in which you like acquire and like

00:24:48.199 --> 00:24:49.000
new information.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:51.839
And so it's like kind of this nice, interesting pairing for folks that are really

00:24:51.839 --> 00:24:57.000
interested in developing new visual representations because you have this really

00:24:57.160 --> 00:25:02.119
abstract data type that you're trying to, you know, come up with new ways to represent

00:25:02.119 --> 00:25:05.359
that are effective for helping understand information.

00:25:05.959 --> 00:25:06.560
Yeah, I see.

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:11.000
So it's less, it might be less about tools necessarily, like rather than tools for

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:17.400
visualization, you could focus on like what visualizations would kind of like help people

00:25:19.079 --> 00:25:22.920
work on these problems or like visualize the space well.

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:26.719
Yeah, I'd say like a pretty like classic example, example would be something like a

00:25:26.719 --> 00:25:27.680
genome browser.

00:25:27.719 --> 00:25:32.119
Like that's like a very, like the types of visual representations in a genome browser

00:25:32.119 --> 00:25:35.520
are like well known, like it's a lot of bars and you know, like dots.

00:25:35.839 --> 00:25:39.880
But then the type of data behind it and like the idea of like overlaying all these

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:44.920
different tracks and it being like zoomable and like panable and like also just laying

00:25:44.920 --> 00:25:46.359
out the genome like linearly.

00:25:46.359 --> 00:25:49.560
Like that's like a chosen representation for that information.

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:55.959
Although what I will say is like from the visualization community, like when I joined,

00:25:55.959 --> 00:26:00.760
I was super excited about, you know, I'd gotten into working on, interested in

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:01.719
visualization.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:06.199
Some of my first like open source contributions were in sort of like visualization

00:26:06.199 --> 00:26:06.959
libraries.

00:26:07.359 --> 00:26:10.839
And I think I was just motivated to have something that like kind of fit my background,

00:26:10.839 --> 00:26:13.280
but where I could study visualization and work on it.

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:19.040
And it seemed like the focus of that community is really on these like novel

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:27.239
representations or because that is where like interesting, I don't know, like that's

00:26:27.239 --> 00:26:28.760
kind of like where the field was.

00:26:28.760 --> 00:26:32.920
And so the types of like applications that we, or the types of software that we would

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:38.439
work on would resemble like a lot more like monolithic like software applications.

00:26:38.439 --> 00:26:40.680
Like it's, hey, we built this like viewer for this.

00:26:41.199 --> 00:26:46.920
And what I found over time was that like we ran into all these practical barriers where

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:51.359
like that might make a really interesting like visualization paper, but like the field

00:26:51.359 --> 00:26:54.599
is sort of interested in these like new representations, but things that were like are

00:26:54.599 --> 00:26:58.400
kind of like boring to the field now, like still are not being used by like practitioners

00:26:58.400 --> 00:26:59.959
that like have their hands on data.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.520
And I think that that's where I really started to think like, or I took for granted

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:06.280
like the cost of the tools that we use.

00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:11.800
Like if you are like, I use this tool and I work on my data every day and then I come

00:27:11.800 --> 00:27:13.839
to you and I say, I made another tool for you.

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:16.719
It's like, okay, does that compliment my workflow?

00:27:16.719 --> 00:27:18.959
Are you asking me to like throw out my previous thing?

00:27:18.959 --> 00:27:20.599
Like, how do I get my data into that?

00:27:20.599 --> 00:27:23.959
Like, these are all just questions that you start to have to either answer with software

00:27:23.959 --> 00:27:27.239
in that application or like workflows to get data in there.

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:32.920
And I think I just got kind of frustrated in this feeling that we, the community that

00:27:32.920 --> 00:27:34.079
felt like it had solutions.

00:27:34.079 --> 00:27:37.359
And like, there was like kind of this disconnect back to like the folks that would

00:27:37.359 --> 00:27:41.959
actually benefit from using them and in understanding their workflows in real ways.

00:27:42.479 --> 00:27:47.719
I remember going to a Viz conference a while ago and having this just like really strong

00:27:47.719 --> 00:27:51.880
feeling, like these people were like, this is like haute couture.

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:57.000
Like they're delivering, they're creating these amazing kind of bespoke pieces of art

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:58.680
that's tailored to one person.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:02.280
And I was coming in and I'm like, I'm like a buyer at Target.

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:06.119
Like, if I can't get this on the back of a million people, like who cares?

00:28:06.239 --> 00:28:10.119
Like, it's just such an interesting like difference in perspective.

00:28:10.959 --> 00:28:13.599
But it just, it feels like that, like that, that.

00:28:13.799 --> 00:28:20.859
— but it just feels like there's much more emphasis these days on actually shipping

00:28:20.859 --> 00:28:26.839
stuff that people — more than the one research group you're carefully collaborating with

00:28:26.839 --> 00:28:28.839
can use.

00:28:28.839 --> 00:28:32.639
I think I started to think about it a lot more, too.

00:28:32.639 --> 00:28:37.358
I think the reason why — it's not like that community set off to build these only specialized

00:28:37.358 --> 00:28:44.779
applications, but in writing my PhD thesis in the end, I tried to think a lot about why

00:28:44.779 --> 00:28:47.918
visualization software is kind of different from other software.

00:28:47.918 --> 00:28:52.319
And I think a part of it is just that it touches so many pieces of the software stack, like

00:28:52.319 --> 00:29:00.398
it has a backend, and it has the actual UI layer, so you can shell out to different commands

00:29:00.398 --> 00:29:04.039
from a lot of different applications, but it's really hard to take this visual thing

00:29:04.039 --> 00:29:07.918
that was built one way and stuff it in another one if they're built with totally different

00:29:07.918 --> 00:29:10.359
technologies.

00:29:10.359 --> 00:29:14.799
So yeah, I think I just started to focus — maybe there are ways that we could improve our software

00:29:14.799 --> 00:29:19.678
practices in such a way that we could try to standardize a little bit more how we're

00:29:19.678 --> 00:29:23.638
building these visual representations, such that we don't need to — one, we don't need

00:29:23.638 --> 00:29:29.199
to build the all-encompassing tool that does everything, but maybe we can layer on just

00:29:29.199 --> 00:29:32.599
the visualization layer into an environment where someone already knows what they're doing

00:29:32.599 --> 00:29:33.599
with their data.

00:29:33.599 --> 00:29:38.479
And that led me to looking at notebooks, because that was something that I used a lot for working

00:29:38.479 --> 00:29:42.839
with data, and also our closest collaborators use Python notebooks.

00:29:42.839 --> 00:29:47.239
And then yeah, also there's a way of extending Jupyter notebooks with something called Jupyter

00:29:47.239 --> 00:29:52.438
Widgets, and that was sort of how I started playing around with this idea of taking web-based

00:29:52.438 --> 00:29:56.979
visualizations and embedding them inside of the environment where people have their hands

00:29:56.979 --> 00:30:01.799
on data rather than saying, hey, here's a new application, and sort of giving that feeling

00:30:01.799 --> 00:30:05.919
of, hey, if there's this library, pip install it and get going, rather than like, oh, go

00:30:05.919 --> 00:30:10.519
to this website, download the software, run this script to export into a particular file

00:30:10.519 --> 00:30:12.038
format and then ingest it.

00:30:12.038 --> 00:30:17.919
I mean, the other thing that did change is you could create — now you can create high-performance

00:30:17.919 --> 00:30:25.679
visualizations in a web browser, and 10 years ago that required C++ code, or 20 years ago.

00:30:25.679 --> 00:30:30.879
That is another — that's one of the reasons that it's so much easier today to distribute

00:30:30.879 --> 00:30:31.879
this stuff.

00:30:31.879 --> 00:30:32.879
Yeah.

00:30:32.879 --> 00:30:33.879
That's fair.

00:30:33.879 --> 00:30:42.039
I guess to the notebook point too, yeah, it's interesting because I — Trevor, you mentioned

00:30:42.039 --> 00:30:46.879
like all of these applications had to worry about how does data come in?

00:30:46.879 --> 00:30:52.599
And I guess if you look at it from the notebook side, it's like this kind of substrate can

00:30:52.599 --> 00:30:55.319
handle that across everyone.

00:30:55.319 --> 00:31:01.358
And it sounds like you're saying if you just focus on the visualization part, the notebook

00:31:01.358 --> 00:31:08.039
can kind of take care of all these activities that every single bespoke app repeats, essentially?

00:31:08.039 --> 00:31:09.519
Yeah.

00:31:09.519 --> 00:31:13.319
The concrete example I like to give is like, imagine that you have an application that

00:31:13.319 --> 00:31:16.519
like renders points, like a scatterplot application.

00:31:16.519 --> 00:31:18.759
And someone's like, it's like, okay, how do I get my data into that?

00:31:18.759 --> 00:31:21.119
It's like, okay, we take a CSV as input.

00:31:21.119 --> 00:31:22.759
Okay, what are the columns of that CSV?

00:31:23.198 --> 00:31:27.599
And then it's like, but what if I had a TSV or what if I had like a parquet file or like,

00:31:27.599 --> 00:31:31.438
it starts to be that now that you're putting stress on that application to solve that layer

00:31:31.438 --> 00:31:35.879
of like, how do I get my data into memory such that I can put it into this visual representation

00:31:35.879 --> 00:31:40.438
versus in an environment like a notebook, you can kind of just say my data visualization

00:31:40.438 --> 00:31:43.558
takes a data frame as input, and it should have these columns.

00:31:43.558 --> 00:31:49.159
And then there's other libraries that someone is interested or that works in notebooks knows

00:31:49.159 --> 00:31:50.918
how to get their data into a data frame.

00:31:51.159 --> 00:31:56.358
And so you can really focus on like, the data types, like tabular versus maybe like multidimensional

00:31:56.358 --> 00:32:00.358
arrays, or I feel like are kind of like the core data types in the data science ecosystem.

00:32:01.078 --> 00:32:05.478
And then if we just build the visualization layers on top of that, you sort of reduce

00:32:05.478 --> 00:32:10.119
the requirement of the front end of having to like understand or do that massaging of

00:32:10.119 --> 00:32:10.599
the data.

00:32:10.599 --> 00:32:16.119
And instead, you can sort of have a much like thinner, like actual visual representation

00:32:16.119 --> 00:32:17.319
that you are contributing.

00:32:17.879 --> 00:32:18.119
Yeah.

00:32:18.119 --> 00:32:22.838
And then the bit that makes that work is you can now rely on the like, you can just expect

00:32:22.838 --> 00:32:29.078
that the end user can like program so they can like stitch together the one package that

00:32:29.078 --> 00:32:35.078
does import with another package does visualization, which again is, yeah, like has not always

00:32:35.078 --> 00:32:38.358
been the case for visualization tools.

00:32:39.078 --> 00:32:39.799
Yeah.

00:32:39.799 --> 00:32:44.198
And I think like the same way that you could, you know, like the libraries like scikit-learn

00:32:44.758 --> 00:32:50.119
don't necessarily import, or I don't actually know if they do, but like have IO primitives

00:32:50.119 --> 00:32:50.439
in them.

00:32:50.439 --> 00:32:53.239
It's more like algorithms that you apply to data structures.

00:32:53.239 --> 00:32:55.559
And, you know, how do you do that for visualizations?

00:32:55.559 --> 00:33:00.758
It's like, maybe we assume similar data structures, but we just do the visualization side and not

00:33:00.758 --> 00:33:02.439
so much of like the pre-processing.

00:33:04.119 --> 00:33:09.959
And in Marimo, just to tie it up with a bow too, in Marimo, this is a widget.

00:33:09.959 --> 00:33:11.878
Is that right?

00:33:12.439 --> 00:33:13.239
Yeah, exactly.

00:33:13.239 --> 00:33:18.679
So, you know, to complete this, I guess, this thread with like Jupyter widgets, I started

00:33:18.679 --> 00:33:22.198
working on them because they seem like this useful primitive for, you know, extending

00:33:22.198 --> 00:33:25.478
notebooks with these interactive elements.

00:33:25.478 --> 00:33:28.999
And just to be concrete, a widget is like a little bit of front end code.

00:33:28.999 --> 00:33:34.119
So something that runs in your web browser that can embed directly into the output area

00:33:34.119 --> 00:33:34.999
inside of a notebook.

00:33:34.999 --> 00:33:39.239
So if you have a cell that runs some code, now you can have like some user defined front

00:33:39.239 --> 00:33:40.279
end code that sits there.

00:33:40.918 --> 00:33:46.519
But the really important bit is that also that code has a way of like communicating

00:33:46.519 --> 00:33:49.638
back to your actual, where your Python objects live.

00:33:49.638 --> 00:33:54.119
And so it provides this really rich interface for actually like, I can move a slider and

00:33:54.119 --> 00:33:58.038
that changes the value that actually lives in Python, not just like in the front end,

00:33:58.038 --> 00:33:59.478
like that state isn't trapped.

00:34:01.399 --> 00:34:05.959
And so, yeah, Jupyter widgets are the mechanism by which Jupyter define like, here's how you

00:34:05.959 --> 00:34:07.478
sort of coordinate these two worlds.

00:34:07.719 --> 00:34:12.119
And what ended up happening was at the, you know, when widgets first came out, you had

00:34:12.119 --> 00:34:16.839
this fragmentation of different front ends for Jupyter where you had like Google Colab

00:34:16.839 --> 00:34:20.758
or VS Code or JupyterLab or even classic Jupyter.

00:34:20.758 --> 00:34:24.918
And because the web browser didn't have like a standard module system, they all kind of

00:34:24.918 --> 00:34:28.758
rolled their own system for like how you load third party JavaScript code.

00:34:28.758 --> 00:34:32.918
And so what that meant was now every widget author like had to become an expert in like

00:34:33.878 --> 00:34:38.279
web bundlers and package managers to be able to like create something that could be used

00:34:38.279 --> 00:34:38.999
universally.

00:34:39.638 --> 00:34:42.758
So I started working on a couple of Jupyter widgets.

00:34:42.758 --> 00:34:47.478
I'd been working on building like, you know, web applications because that's how we built

00:34:47.478 --> 00:34:49.318
a lot of the visualization tools.

00:34:49.318 --> 00:34:51.959
It just felt like a lot different type of development.

00:34:51.959 --> 00:34:56.999
Like it felt like it was like years before what I was familiar with, with kind of with

00:34:56.999 --> 00:34:58.598
modern web development.

00:34:58.598 --> 00:35:03.638
So AnyWidget, which is the project that came out of this, was originally just a way for

00:35:03.638 --> 00:35:07.559
me to be able to iterate very quickly on like building like more of these and make it a

00:35:07.559 --> 00:35:10.999
little bit more fun where I didn't have to like restart my browser and like refresh everything

00:35:10.999 --> 00:35:11.959
to like see the updates.

00:35:11.959 --> 00:35:17.478
It was trying to come up with unifying like the way that you wrote that front end code

00:35:17.478 --> 00:35:22.679
more around how modern browsers talk today and then like ingesting that in a bunch of

00:35:22.679 --> 00:35:23.399
different environments.

00:35:23.399 --> 00:35:29.239
So I could sort of solve the platform compatibility problem once and then like just author this

00:35:29.239 --> 00:35:30.918
thing that's a little bit more portable.

00:35:31.638 --> 00:35:36.358
Just for some context too, I think people, like a lot of people think of AnyWidget today,

00:35:36.358 --> 00:35:44.279
but yeah, having lived through the shift to AnyWidget, yeah, I can't overstate how convenient

00:35:44.279 --> 00:35:49.478
it is to have something run across all these different platforms that I do feel like that

00:35:49.478 --> 00:35:53.239
juggling was real of figuring out like I can run in the Jupyter notebook.

00:35:55.078 --> 00:35:59.959
Now I've done the work to figure it out for Google Colab, but like to have it in one place

00:35:59.959 --> 00:36:04.918
and not even have to worry about that is so convenient, seems like.

00:36:05.959 --> 00:36:06.598
Yeah.

00:36:06.598 --> 00:36:12.598
And I think also like, you know, when I've used notebooks for prototyping like Python

00:36:12.598 --> 00:36:17.959
code, like maybe to speak more concretely, like I use notebooks to prototype code because

00:36:17.959 --> 00:36:20.598
it's a way that I can run little snippets of code and try them out.

00:36:20.598 --> 00:36:24.519
But in order previously to like even just experiment with the idea of like a visual

00:36:24.519 --> 00:36:29.239
output, like a widget, like you'd have to like create a new Python project and like

00:36:29.239 --> 00:36:32.598
kind of like link and like start this like build process in Bundler.

00:36:32.598 --> 00:36:36.519
And so like you couldn't just like play around with, hey, maybe it'd be fun to like draw

00:36:36.519 --> 00:36:39.158
these things in the web and put them there.

00:36:39.158 --> 00:36:45.078
And that was probably the most like exciting thing to me originally with AnyWidget was

00:36:45.078 --> 00:36:48.038
just the fact that inside of a notebook, I could like start playing with both front

00:36:48.038 --> 00:36:52.278
end and my Python code together and then kind of get these proof of concepts very quickly

00:36:52.278 --> 00:36:54.358
without having to like start a whole new project.

00:36:54.999 --> 00:37:00.918
So does Marimo like extend the Jupyter widget protocol in some way, or is it just much better

00:37:00.918 --> 00:37:01.958
because it's not reactive?

00:37:01.958 --> 00:37:05.958
So when one widget changes, the others can update automatically.

00:37:06.598 --> 00:37:07.718
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:37:07.718 --> 00:37:14.999
So part of this is that AnyWidget itself defines a specification, not only for like the end

00:37:14.999 --> 00:37:20.038
the people that author widgets, but also like what a host platform has to implement

00:37:20.038 --> 00:37:22.439
in terms of APIs to support widgets.

00:37:22.439 --> 00:37:28.119
So if you're using AnyWidget in a traditional Jupyter notebook, that protocol layer is like

00:37:28.119 --> 00:37:31.879
the host platform, AnyWidget, the library, like plugs that in so that you can just load

00:37:31.879 --> 00:37:33.879
them and like run in those environments.

00:37:33.879 --> 00:37:40.439
But inside of Marimo, we actually like natively implement an AnyWidget API to like support

00:37:40.439 --> 00:37:41.559
the AnyWidget protocol.

00:37:41.559 --> 00:37:46.679
So there is no like Jupyter machinery inside of Marimo, but they do support AnyWidget.

00:37:46.679 --> 00:37:50.358
So this thing that sort of started originally as a way for me to be able to author like

00:37:50.358 --> 00:37:54.838
traditional Jupyter widgets, and now is like if you author them there, there are even more

00:37:54.838 --> 00:37:58.999
platforms beyond Jupyter because if they can implement sort of the host side of that front

00:37:58.999 --> 00:38:03.639
end code, now you have this contract where basically something's going to load this and

00:38:03.639 --> 00:38:05.079
render these arguments.

00:38:05.079 --> 00:38:09.958
And then you can expect these APIs to exist is basically the runtime contract.

00:38:11.559 --> 00:38:17.718
It's been interesting to see too that a lot of tools like used to have these like catches,

00:38:17.718 --> 00:38:19.799
like if I'm in Jupyter, do this.

00:38:19.799 --> 00:38:21.158
If I'm in Colab, do this.

00:38:21.799 --> 00:38:27.478
It's been interesting to see like a lot of big tools switch to just using AnyWidget like

00:38:27.478 --> 00:38:33.639
that from that kind of like if else block of like what environment am I in?

00:38:33.639 --> 00:38:39.398
But from what you're saying, Marimo also uses AnyWidget as kind of its native like widget

00:38:39.958 --> 00:38:41.079
implementation, is that?

00:38:41.639 --> 00:38:46.518
Yeah, if you want to make custom interactive elements, any Marimo has special machinery

00:38:46.518 --> 00:38:48.038
to host AnyWidgets.

00:38:48.038 --> 00:38:54.119
And then like the widgets themselves sort of have this internal state on the Python side

00:38:54.119 --> 00:38:57.158
that is currently like based off of this library called traitlets.

00:38:57.158 --> 00:39:01.079
That's just the Python side is like the AnyWidget or the machinery.

00:39:02.358 --> 00:39:08.038
And what you can do is like pluck off individual pieces of state from those widgets to observe

00:39:08.199 --> 00:39:10.759
as reactive values inside of your notebook.

00:39:10.759 --> 00:39:16.598
So I'll declare my widget, I can use a decorator that's called like, it's like, or a function

00:39:16.598 --> 00:39:19.958
is called mo.ui.AnyWidget and wrap my widget in that.

00:39:19.958 --> 00:39:24.038
And that will just listen to every single value on that widget.

00:39:24.038 --> 00:39:28.679
Or you can take individual pieces of state and lift them off of the widget as well.

00:39:28.679 --> 00:39:32.918
And now you basically are like taking that internal reactivity and pulling those into

00:39:32.918 --> 00:39:34.119
Marimo's reactive graph.

00:39:34.278 --> 00:39:39.879
So now I can like reference a value in a different cell, and it plays nicely by Marimo's rules.

00:39:40.439 --> 00:39:46.838
I'm curious too, I know you mentioned you've been working more lately on Marimo pair.

00:39:47.879 --> 00:39:53.319
Maybe tell us a little bit about Marimo pair and the problem that solves.

00:39:54.679 --> 00:40:01.639
Yeah, I'd say, you know, increasingly, we've started, I started using coding agents at

00:40:02.598 --> 00:40:05.398
work for doing like traditional software development.

00:40:06.358 --> 00:40:12.679
And we had like some initial sort of integrations with like coding agents for Marimo, but both

00:40:12.679 --> 00:40:17.239
from our users and like my own personal experience, like there felt just kind of something off

00:40:17.239 --> 00:40:19.239
when you tried to like do something in notebooks.

00:40:19.239 --> 00:40:24.038
Like it was like, and I couldn't really put my thumb on it at the time, but it was just

00:40:24.038 --> 00:40:26.518
like, okay, I have this data idea.

00:40:26.518 --> 00:40:31.958
And, you know, with my coding agent, I can just let it loose in like a folder of a bunch

00:40:31.958 --> 00:40:35.799
of script or on my file system and just like let it rip on a problem.

00:40:35.799 --> 00:40:38.518
And I'll come back and I'll have a bunch of scripts and some outputs.

00:40:39.239 --> 00:40:43.879
But like if I tried to use like integrations I'd tried with other types of notebooks, or

00:40:43.879 --> 00:40:49.398
even with Marimo at that time, you'd like, the way that our integration worked then was

00:40:49.398 --> 00:40:54.598
sort of, we'd edit the like the Python file on disk and try to like play the different

00:40:55.319 --> 00:40:56.838
changes back inside the UI.

00:40:57.718 --> 00:41:00.918
And what would happen is the, you know, you'd let the agent go and it would just kind of

00:41:00.918 --> 00:41:02.358
say like, okay, good, I'm done.

00:41:02.358 --> 00:41:03.319
Like it works.

00:41:03.319 --> 00:41:05.478
And like, it like wasn't working.

00:41:05.478 --> 00:41:07.799
There would be some like issue in the UI.

00:41:08.358 --> 00:41:12.119
And then you really felt like you're kind of like babysitting this agent, which is like

00:41:12.119 --> 00:41:16.518
a much different user experience to like working on traditional software where it's like, hey,

00:41:16.518 --> 00:41:18.759
I have these tests, go run them until you like fix the problem.

00:41:18.759 --> 00:41:18.999
Right.

00:41:18.999 --> 00:41:22.918
Or like it's going to encounter syntax errors and like keep going until it like finishes

00:41:22.918 --> 00:41:23.418
up.

00:41:24.679 --> 00:41:29.799
So yeah, Marimo pair kind of spawned out of like taking a step back at how we were approaching,

00:41:29.799 --> 00:41:33.478
like trying to drive, like use notebooks with agents.

00:41:34.038 --> 00:41:39.079
And I really think our new framing of it is thinking about like notebooks, you know, notebooks

00:41:39.079 --> 00:41:42.838
and stateful environments for coding have been useful for humans to understand data.

00:41:43.398 --> 00:41:48.119
Is there a way that we can like actually make notebooks useful for agents as well for exploring

00:41:48.119 --> 00:41:49.158
data problems?

00:41:49.158 --> 00:41:53.478
And so it was not so much as like, can we use like a notebook as nice context for the

00:41:53.478 --> 00:41:57.158
model, but like, can the model actually just use a notebook to like reason and like work

00:41:57.158 --> 00:41:58.439
on a problem for a while?

00:41:58.439 --> 00:42:04.679
And that, that changed, that led to us like really changing how we went about like exposing

00:42:04.679 --> 00:42:06.278
our notebooks as a tool for agents.

00:42:06.999 --> 00:42:07.239
Yeah.

00:42:07.239 --> 00:42:12.999
I demoed Marimo pair for the company at like a work week because the fact that in Marimo,

00:42:12.999 --> 00:42:18.838
it can just, the agent seems to be able to do anything, kind of like anything I could

00:42:18.838 --> 00:42:19.879
imagine clicking.

00:42:19.879 --> 00:42:25.879
It seems like the agent can do, I don't, there may be some limitations, but it's, it's been

00:42:25.879 --> 00:42:28.358
so interesting to interact with.

00:42:29.958 --> 00:42:34.598
Yeah, I'd say like previously, or the way that I'd read about using something like MCP

00:42:34.598 --> 00:42:38.838
is like, it's, you know, model context protocol is this way of like taking an application

00:42:38.838 --> 00:42:41.478
and like exposing it to like your agent.

00:42:42.119 --> 00:42:46.358
And the way that we were using MCP before was kind of like allowing the agent to query

00:42:46.358 --> 00:42:47.879
the state of the notebook.

00:42:47.879 --> 00:42:50.598
So it'd be things like, you know, what are the cells?

00:42:50.598 --> 00:42:52.199
What are the contents of the cells?

00:42:52.999 --> 00:42:57.799
Edit, you know, what are my variables that are inside of the notebook?

00:42:57.799 --> 00:43:01.478
But then what you, what we realized was like, you know, when the agent would take an action

00:43:01.478 --> 00:43:05.559
like writing or mute, like doing something in the notebook, it was using its existing

00:43:05.559 --> 00:43:09.879
tools to edit a file on disk, but it never got feedback for like, Hey, did something

00:43:09.879 --> 00:43:10.518
go wrong?

00:43:10.518 --> 00:43:12.598
Like in that same execution channel.

00:43:12.598 --> 00:43:16.918
So it was kind of, so what the agent would have to get good at is like making a change

00:43:16.918 --> 00:43:20.838
and then querying the notebook of like, and making a bunch of serial queries to be like,

00:43:20.838 --> 00:43:21.799
did something go wrong?

00:43:22.439 --> 00:43:26.598
And what we changed inside of Marimo pair is we basically got rid of like all of our

00:43:26.598 --> 00:43:27.319
tools.

00:43:27.319 --> 00:43:30.199
And we just have one tool that is like run Python code.

00:43:30.999 --> 00:43:33.478
And that just runs Python code in the Marimo kernel.

00:43:34.199 --> 00:43:38.598
And there's a hidden like Python API in there where the agent is able to control the Marimo

00:43:38.598 --> 00:43:40.999
notebook and ask about the Marimo notebook itself.

00:43:40.999 --> 00:43:46.838
And so when it does something like hallucinate a value or, you know, redefine a variable

00:43:46.838 --> 00:43:50.918
or something that doesn't play nicely by Marimo's rules or Python's rules, it just gets feedback

00:43:50.918 --> 00:43:54.358
in that same like turn that says, Hey, you need to change that.

00:43:54.358 --> 00:43:55.398
Like that's not working.

00:43:55.398 --> 00:43:56.439
There's an error.

00:43:56.439 --> 00:43:57.639
And then it can keep going.

00:43:57.639 --> 00:44:04.278
And that ability to, I think, have both that read only access and like see the effects

00:44:04.278 --> 00:44:08.439
of the mutations, like a very similar way to like when you edit files on disk and then

00:44:08.439 --> 00:44:11.559
run the scripts to see if they, you know, exit code one or zero.

00:44:12.119 --> 00:44:14.518
Now the agent can do that at the cell level.

00:44:14.518 --> 00:44:19.958
And that, I think, allows for this ability for, you know, the usefulness of notebooks

00:44:19.958 --> 00:44:21.398
is that you can iterate very quickly.

00:44:21.398 --> 00:44:26.038
And then when you try to like give agents to them previously, it started to feel like,

00:44:26.038 --> 00:44:29.239
OK, but the agent isn't being very productive in this environment.

00:44:29.239 --> 00:44:32.838
And I think that that was sort of the missing piece was like, how do you give them feedback

00:44:32.838 --> 00:44:36.918
in that same execution channel to seeing, you know, what you're seeing so that you don't

00:44:36.918 --> 00:44:39.079
have to sit there and tell it that something went wrong.

00:44:39.639 --> 00:44:45.239
It is interesting that like data exploration is like so stateful.

00:44:45.239 --> 00:44:49.319
When you think about like traditional software engineering, like you run your unit tests

00:44:49.319 --> 00:44:53.879
and those are all designed in such a way that you start from a fresh session, they run and

00:44:53.879 --> 00:44:55.158
you get the results back.

00:44:55.158 --> 00:45:00.038
But like that just doesn't work for data analysis because it's so iterative.

00:45:00.038 --> 00:45:06.038
And like, yeah, you want to be able to look at the state of the thing you just did and

00:45:06.038 --> 00:45:08.119
you don't have to rerun the whole thing from scratch.

00:45:08.119 --> 00:45:10.999
Every time we'll figure out some complicated like caching mechanism.

00:45:10.999 --> 00:45:15.958
You just you want to have this like long running persistent session that records like what

00:45:15.958 --> 00:45:17.799
have you done to the data?

00:45:18.599 --> 00:45:19.559
It's interesting.

00:45:19.559 --> 00:45:23.638
And one like interesting property, I think, of having a reactive kernel is that we don't

00:45:23.638 --> 00:45:29.079
allow for these sort of so we don't allow for things where you like can, you know, inside

00:45:29.079 --> 00:45:33.799
of if marimo pair, if the agent chooses to change a cell upstream, it will see the effects

00:45:33.799 --> 00:45:36.199
of like the downstream cells of making that change.

00:45:37.638 --> 00:45:42.998
So it sort of adds this extra layer of guardrails beyond just a normal Python REPL, or in order

00:45:42.998 --> 00:45:47.238
for the agent to like continue on a problem, it has to check cells into the notebook and

00:45:47.238 --> 00:45:48.599
define variables.

00:45:48.599 --> 00:45:51.319
And then each of those cells has to play by marimo's rules.

00:45:51.319 --> 00:45:57.238
So it's almost like, you know, yeah, traditional software development is very stateless, but

00:45:57.238 --> 00:45:59.319
your state is kind of like your file system, right?

00:45:59.319 --> 00:46:02.918
So you like version that over time, and then it's running these like scripts on top of

00:46:02.918 --> 00:46:04.518
this state that you version over time.

00:46:05.638 --> 00:46:10.599
And I think very similarly, we want to provide an experience like that in a stateful environment

00:46:10.599 --> 00:46:15.079
with these guardrails around like, how you have to check those cells in.

00:46:15.079 --> 00:46:20.038
So, you know, in marimo pair, you can't check in a cell that redefines a variable or something.

00:46:20.038 --> 00:46:22.599
So it has to play nicely by marimo's rules.

00:46:22.599 --> 00:46:27.799
And so sort of as a side effect of the agent exploring a problem using marimo, you get

00:46:27.799 --> 00:46:30.278
like a reproducible kind of like execution flow.

00:46:30.839 --> 00:46:33.319
There's a downside to that though, too, though, right?

00:46:33.319 --> 00:46:37.719
Like, like what happens if like the thing at the end of the very end of the reactive

00:46:37.719 --> 00:46:39.799
graph takes like a minute to run?

00:46:39.799 --> 00:46:46.518
Like, how do you, like, I assume marimo must have like tools to like, when you don't want

00:46:46.518 --> 00:46:49.959
things to run all the time to like opt out of opt out of that.

00:46:50.599 --> 00:46:50.839
Yeah.

00:46:50.839 --> 00:46:55.799
So the contract in marimo is that what you see on the screen is a representation of the

00:46:55.799 --> 00:46:56.918
state in the kernel.

00:46:56.918 --> 00:47:01.559
And so you can turn off reactivity or disable individual cells, and they will just show

00:47:01.559 --> 00:47:02.599
as being stale.

00:47:02.599 --> 00:47:04.998
And then you have to like manually execute them.

00:47:04.998 --> 00:47:09.959
So if I turned on like lazy mode, then the agent would get feedback saying you need to

00:47:09.959 --> 00:47:13.158
rerun these cells, but then it would, it could go back and run those cells.

00:47:13.799 --> 00:47:14.599
It's interesting.

00:47:15.398 --> 00:47:21.079
And what you're describing, I think for the agent, as I understand is marimo code mode.

00:47:21.079 --> 00:47:21.799
Is that right?

00:47:21.799 --> 00:47:27.959
The sort of secret backdoor into marimo that an agent can use to basically like create

00:47:27.959 --> 00:47:29.238
cells or edit them.

00:47:31.319 --> 00:47:38.038
And I think another thing you mentioned is like the connection to marimo that like, is

00:47:38.038 --> 00:47:38.359
it right?

00:47:38.359 --> 00:47:44.599
If, if the agent like deletes a cell that defined a variable, it's like the variable

00:47:44.599 --> 00:47:46.439
itself is removed.

00:47:46.439 --> 00:47:51.479
Is that, that's kind of part of the maintaining like a fresh state.

00:47:51.479 --> 00:47:58.918
So it's like, in a way it kind of like locks in the agent into having like an up-to-date

00:47:58.918 --> 00:47:59.799
environment.

00:47:59.799 --> 00:48:03.799
And I don't know, I thought that was really intuitive that like, if I, as a human also

00:48:03.799 --> 00:48:09.238
deleted a cell that defined the variable, I would also kind of want it to go away.

00:48:09.238 --> 00:48:15.158
Like that's a really classic human blunder to like delete the ref, delete the code that

00:48:15.158 --> 00:48:18.439
made it, but still have it like floating around in the environment.

00:48:18.998 --> 00:48:26.839
That seemed like kind of a neat contract to enforce that if the code's gone, that, that

00:48:26.839 --> 00:48:29.079
variable is gone as well.

00:48:29.799 --> 00:48:30.299
Yeah.

00:48:30.839 --> 00:48:35.398
Like it's, it's, it's a hidden Python API that the agent, like if you read the docs

00:48:35.398 --> 00:48:37.959
inside of marimo, it's like, do not use this like yourself.

00:48:37.959 --> 00:48:39.079
If you're a human being.

00:48:39.079 --> 00:48:39.959
If you're a human being.

00:48:39.959 --> 00:48:43.559
And part of that is that we, you know, we don't introduce in comparison to other APIs

00:48:43.559 --> 00:48:48.679
that we have inside of marimo that we want people to like depend on like code mode was

00:48:48.679 --> 00:48:51.638
something that we've like refined over time and we're still like changing.

00:48:51.638 --> 00:48:56.758
And that's part of that is because like, because it's used within these sessions, the agent

00:48:56.758 --> 00:49:01.398
learns about like what APIs it has available and how to call those in that session versus,

00:49:01.398 --> 00:49:05.878
and that's a much different contract between like a model and like software versus like

00:49:05.878 --> 00:49:08.839
two pieces of software where you have like versioning problems.

00:49:08.839 --> 00:49:12.278
And like, you really have to make sure that they yeah.

00:49:12.278 --> 00:49:16.038
Like if someone was building around code mode that was outside of using agents, like

00:49:16.038 --> 00:49:19.719
they would get very annoyed with us when we made breaking changes versus the model basically

00:49:19.719 --> 00:49:23.638
gets a new version of marimo and it has new capabilities or maybe we fix bugs and it just

00:49:23.638 --> 00:49:27.319
calls those APIs and can sort of chug along inside the notebook.

00:49:27.319 --> 00:49:31.479
Presumably code mode is what the marimo UI uses behind the scenes.

00:49:32.118 --> 00:49:37.719
So most of marimo's UI is driven through like the front end, which is like JavaScript code.

00:49:37.719 --> 00:49:40.998
So we've had a lot of like, you know, it's this message, I mean, to get technical, it's

00:49:40.998 --> 00:49:44.038
like this message patching system of like edits, going back to the kernel, you have

00:49:44.038 --> 00:49:46.118
some piece of state that you're updating.

00:49:47.158 --> 00:49:51.878
But really we had to introduce like a Python API for driving marimo notebooks because,

00:49:52.518 --> 00:49:53.799
which we didn't really have before.

00:49:53.799 --> 00:49:56.758
And that made us changing a little bit about how our document model works.

00:49:56.758 --> 00:50:00.439
So now you can like drive it from Python or you can drive it from the front end and they

00:50:00.439 --> 00:50:02.278
can all like sort of share that state.

00:50:02.278 --> 00:50:08.359
But you know, really the reason we were able to do that originally was just because our

00:50:08.359 --> 00:50:09.878
kernel is in Python.

00:50:10.038 --> 00:50:14.518
So when I first worked on marimo pair, the first thing introduced was just the ability

00:50:14.518 --> 00:50:16.599
for the model to like run Python code in the kernel.

00:50:16.599 --> 00:50:20.118
And that let it do basically everything that was like read-only inside of marimo.

00:50:20.118 --> 00:50:23.878
So it's like, you know, we have this big set of MCP tools that were like list my data frame,

00:50:23.878 --> 00:50:25.238
list my variables.

00:50:25.238 --> 00:50:26.758
And now it's like, okay, get rid of all those.

00:50:26.758 --> 00:50:31.158
You just write the Python code you want to like cat out a table, like print the columns,

00:50:31.158 --> 00:50:35.479
like you can discover APIs, like just call Python the same way.

00:50:35.479 --> 00:50:38.518
If you're running an agent and you see it using Python from the command line, it's like

00:50:38.518 --> 00:50:40.359
running scripts like very quickly.

00:50:41.799 --> 00:50:45.158
But then, and then I think the first thing I did was like, okay, show me a notification

00:50:45.158 --> 00:50:45.719
in the UI.

00:50:46.359 --> 00:50:48.758
And I saw it like, like try a bunch of different things.

00:50:48.758 --> 00:50:51.959
And then finally it found like one API where it like toasted me a notification.

00:50:51.959 --> 00:50:52.758
I was like, whoa, okay.

00:50:52.758 --> 00:50:53.799
Like, how'd you do that?

00:50:53.799 --> 00:50:55.638
And then like, we kind of like look back through the code.

00:50:56.758 --> 00:51:00.278
And so we started to then think about like what types of things would you want to drive

00:51:00.278 --> 00:51:01.559
from the kernel?

00:51:01.559 --> 00:51:06.118
And then we harden those behind like a Python API that we could test and such that, you

00:51:06.679 --> 00:51:09.479
know, you can have high confidence when the agent doesn't have to like get the right sequencing

00:51:09.479 --> 00:51:10.278
of notifications.

00:51:10.278 --> 00:51:11.319
It just calls an API.

00:51:11.319 --> 00:51:15.959
And then we replay those notifications to the front end and also make sure the kernel

00:51:15.959 --> 00:51:16.679
stays up to date.

00:51:17.238 --> 00:51:18.518
Yeah, it's such an interesting approach.

00:51:18.518 --> 00:51:24.118
And also I do think it's interesting to hear it in contrast to like the MCP or tools.

00:51:24.118 --> 00:51:26.679
There's like a lot of ways to kind of carve this up.

00:51:26.679 --> 00:51:32.038
But I think we've like broad, like in the early phases of like MCP being a thing, everyone

00:51:32.038 --> 00:51:38.599
was like, let's make a bajillion MCP tools and then kind of discovered like, oh, actually

00:51:38.599 --> 00:51:43.479
LLMs are like way better at writing code in an existing language than trying to figure

00:51:43.479 --> 00:51:48.038
out some like bizarre novel set of tools that you have created.

00:51:48.038 --> 00:51:54.359
And now like, I think, I mean, MCP still has a, as it plays a role, but it's so much more

00:51:54.359 --> 00:51:58.758
powerful just to give it a few like tools that let it write code and do stuff like that's

00:51:59.479 --> 00:52:00.758
really, really powerful.

00:52:01.238 --> 00:52:05.638
Like we basically allow it to do, like, we still have a way of running Marimo pair through

00:52:05.638 --> 00:52:09.158
MCP, but there's only one tool and it's like executing Python code.

00:52:10.439 --> 00:52:16.679
And I think the other thing that was non-trivial to me that when we changed that way of having

00:52:16.679 --> 00:52:21.719
many tools to like one very sharp tool is that programming languages have this, you

00:52:21.719 --> 00:52:24.679
know, ability to like compose different primitives as well.

00:52:24.679 --> 00:52:28.679
So like you don't have a bunch of different tool calls, you could actually in one execute

00:52:28.679 --> 00:52:29.319
code.

00:52:29.319 --> 00:52:30.758
It could like look at the notebook state.

00:52:30.758 --> 00:52:32.998
It could like create multiple cells, delete cells.

00:52:32.998 --> 00:52:38.359
Like if you don't have, those don't have to be individual transactions that you replay.

00:52:38.359 --> 00:52:42.918
And instead it's like, you can leave it up to the creativity of the model to figure out

00:52:42.918 --> 00:52:46.679
how to string these things together to accomplish the task that the user wants to do inside

00:52:46.679 --> 00:52:47.238
the notebook.

00:52:47.799 --> 00:52:47.959
Yeah.

00:52:47.959 --> 00:52:53.559
I see that a lot when using Claude to write data analysis code, like it will write the

00:52:53.638 --> 00:53:00.998
R code to ask, answer like three questions at once, rather than having to, yeah, like

00:53:00.998 --> 00:53:03.638
ask us then, yeah, like what is the data?

00:53:03.638 --> 00:53:04.599
What are the data frames?

00:53:04.599 --> 00:53:05.799
What are the variables in the data frame?

00:53:05.799 --> 00:53:06.518
What are their types?

00:53:06.518 --> 00:53:11.559
Just does that all at one, one swoop and gives a bunch of information back to the model.

00:53:12.998 --> 00:53:19.238
And I feel like just to set the stage too, for what, like when I use Marimo pair that

00:53:19.238 --> 00:53:22.439
there's a skill, like essentially it was so interesting to be like,

00:53:22.998 --> 00:53:24.278
okay, just use this skill.

00:53:25.079 --> 00:53:29.959
I was using Claude code from the command line, just like plug it in and that Claude code

00:53:29.959 --> 00:53:36.038
actually could, I mean, and this isn't a surprise, install Marimo, you know, essentially

00:53:36.038 --> 00:53:44.278
fire it up on my computer, tab opens in my browser, and then just starts driving Marimo.

00:53:44.998 --> 00:53:50.679
I feel like that was such a kind of eyeopening experience to realize like there's another

00:53:50.679 --> 00:53:56.518
world where Marimo said like, Hey, set us up, you know, run the agent from inside Marimo

00:53:57.238 --> 00:53:58.599
and it could do some stuff.

00:53:58.599 --> 00:54:03.638
But to watch it actually like run, realize that we needed a blob on the outside, like

00:54:03.638 --> 00:54:12.518
even running Marimo, opening it and orchestrating it, uh, was a really interesting experience

00:54:12.518 --> 00:54:13.799
to, to see.

00:54:14.518 --> 00:54:19.398
And the other thing I'll flag in, in correct me if I'm wrong, is that Claude code is

00:54:19.479 --> 00:54:22.839
essentially using a CLI, like a bash tool.

00:54:22.839 --> 00:54:23.559
Is that right?

00:54:23.559 --> 00:54:31.878
To basically like ship this Python code in, this Marimo code mode in, to run in the kernel.

00:54:31.878 --> 00:54:33.799
Is that, is that right?

00:54:34.679 --> 00:54:34.998
Yeah.

00:54:34.998 --> 00:54:40.278
So if you install the skill from like GitHub, which is the way that we recommend doing it,

00:54:40.278 --> 00:54:43.719
you'll get a bash script that the agent can call that's called execute code.

00:54:44.278 --> 00:54:49.398
And behind the scenes, what that does is it pings an API endpoint on Marimo that we have

00:54:49.398 --> 00:54:55.079
set up that then replays or kind of maps the, that HTTP response back to your standard end,

00:54:55.079 --> 00:54:59.398
standard out, um, standard error, and then exit with an exit code of zero and one.

00:54:59.959 --> 00:55:04.118
Um, and what's cool about that is if, you know, so we have a cloud hosted version of

00:55:04.118 --> 00:55:08.359
