00:55:08.359 --> 00:55:09.158
the cloud.

00:55:09.158 --> 00:55:13.319
And because it's all done over HTTP, you could actually point Marimo pair to like talk to a

00:55:13.319 --> 00:55:17.479
remote version of, um, of your Marimo that's running.

00:55:17.479 --> 00:55:19.719
And then you don't even need to have Marimo installed locally.

00:55:19.719 --> 00:55:21.638
You just need curl and bash scripts.

00:55:21.638 --> 00:55:25.878
So then you can be using cloud and like you're driving a remote notebook as well.

00:55:25.878 --> 00:55:29.799
So in terms of like the distribution ergonomics, that has been like really nice in that

00:55:30.518 --> 00:55:33.559
you can sort of like reduce the system dependency based off of like where people

00:55:33.559 --> 00:55:36.118
want to be running the Marimo instance.

00:55:36.118 --> 00:55:36.918
It's interesting.

00:55:36.918 --> 00:55:42.439
Cause we, I think we, in some, yeah, cause you can also do the opposite, right?

00:55:42.439 --> 00:55:46.599
You can provide a CLI, like you could have a Marimo CLI.

