It's a subcommand in the Marimo CLI that, like, basically is like a prompt that says,

00:56:57.793 --> 00:57:01.353
like, check to see if the — like, we check to see if you have the skill installed.

00:57:01.353 --> 00:57:04.713
If you don't, then we'll, like, prompt Claude to, like, go get the skill and, like, have

00:57:04.713 --> 00:57:07.152
you install it.

00:57:07.152 --> 00:57:11.873
But if you ever — if you look at the Marimo pair skill, it also is, like, very uncoupled

00:57:11.873 --> 00:57:13.632
to, like, the code mode internals.

00:57:13.632 --> 00:57:18.112
Like, it basically tries to orient the agent around, like, the things that Michael was

00:57:18.112 --> 00:57:19.112
talking about.

00:57:19.112 --> 00:57:23.472
Like, you know, if a user's trying to do something with Marimo, like, what's a nice path forward

00:57:23.472 --> 00:57:26.312
and, like, trying to help them, like, spin up Marimo.

00:57:26.312 --> 00:57:30.673
And then the second piece is basically telling it, you have one tool, and then there's this

00:57:30.673 --> 00:57:32.353
thing that's called code mode.

00:57:32.353 --> 00:57:37.273
And, like, when you start a session, go look and see what code mode is in that instance

00:57:37.273 --> 00:57:38.392
of Marimo that you have.

00:57:38.392 --> 00:57:43.873
And so because we can ship changes to code mode, baking in, like, those API invocations

00:57:43.873 --> 00:57:47.432
in the skill itself would kind of lead to this versioning problem.

00:57:47.432 --> 00:57:50.913
And instead, what we've been able to do is, like, version the two independently, where

00:57:50.913 --> 00:57:54.513
we just say, here's the skill, and then we can ship upgrades to Marimo, and we don't

00:57:54.513 --> 00:57:58.752
have to worry about the two sort of, like, drifting from one another.

00:57:58.752 --> 00:58:05.072
It is fascinating to me how agents can change the API ergonomics.

00:58:05.072 --> 00:58:11.833
You know, like, one thing I think that's kind of interesting and surprising is, like, I

00:58:11.833 --> 00:58:16.472
would say on average, like, agents are way better at reading error messages and actioning

00:58:16.472 --> 00:58:18.913
them than humans.

00:58:18.913 --> 00:58:23.352
So like, good error messages have always been important, but they're almost more important

00:58:23.352 --> 00:58:28.193
now because you can assume that an agent will actually read it comprehensively, and

00:58:28.193 --> 00:58:30.112
if you have a good suggestion, it will follow it.

00:58:30.112 --> 00:58:34.752
And then that can make, like, APIs, like, way more discoverable on the fly, because

00:58:34.752 --> 00:58:39.593
as long as the agent kind of gets somewhere in the vicinity, it then gets, like, pointed

00:58:39.593 --> 00:58:41.393
to the right place.

00:58:41.393 --> 00:58:46.753
Yeah, I'd say we've even thought now about, you know, as, like, when you're in a code

00:58:46.753 --> 00:58:52.432
mode execution, having ways of streaming, like, agent-only logs or, like, suggestions

00:58:52.513 --> 00:58:55.912
so that, like, a code path that goes down that, like, hey, you're probably, like, trying

00:58:55.912 --> 00:58:58.792
to do this thing that doesn't really work, or, like, there's a better option.

00:58:58.792 --> 00:59:03.393
Like, that's something we could, like, from Remo, say, actually use this API, and, like,

00:59:03.393 --> 00:59:07.753
that, like, the agents do follow that really well, because you can sort of do this, like,

00:59:07.753 --> 00:59:12.272
in-context learning almost of, yeah, actually do this instead, and as you were saying, they

00:59:12.272 --> 00:59:13.272
follow it quite well.

00:59:13.272 --> 00:59:18.632
Because the other interesting technique is to just implement the API that the LLM hallucinates,

00:59:18.632 --> 00:59:21.272
which I think is also, like, a fascinating API design technique.

00:59:21.272 --> 00:59:25.192
I would say that that was how, like, the early development of code mode was, because

00:59:25.192 --> 00:59:30.033
I had sort of this idea of what I wanted that API to be, and then we ended up introducing

