01:03:02.673 --> 01:03:06.872
Like almost putting this existential question of, like, should we work on notebooks?

01:03:06.872 --> 01:03:14.032
And I think similar to how, you know, we've got, like, humans have reached for tools,

01:03:14.032 --> 01:03:17.872
like clearly notebooks or some sort of, like, live programming environment has been useful

01:03:17.872 --> 01:03:20.453
for humans to work on data problems.

01:03:20.453 --> 01:03:24.792
And I think we're, we're sort of continuing the pursuit that if we give the right ergonomics,

01:03:24.792 --> 01:03:28.953
these can also be useful tools for agents to work on problems.

01:03:28.953 --> 01:03:33.632
And part of that just comes from this, like, ability to iterate on the problem, right?

01:03:33.632 --> 01:03:39.433
Like being able to, a lot of, like, data tasks involve this, I think, greater interplay at

01:03:39.433 --> 01:03:44.032
times between, like, you know, the code you write and the inspection of the outputs and

01:03:44.032 --> 01:03:45.233
then deciding what to do next.

01:03:45.233 --> 01:03:49.192
Like, it can be a little bit more hands-on in the type of work that you're doing and

01:03:49.192 --> 01:03:54.032
giving the agent the ability to, the environment that it's doing that work in, bring in the

01:03:54.032 --> 01:03:56.752
human with, like, a very rich canvas, right?

01:03:56.752 --> 01:04:02.433
Like, like you, Marimo, our notebook itself has the ability to embed these interactive

01:04:02.433 --> 01:04:05.953
outputs or just even just plot, like, static plots, right?

01:04:05.953 --> 01:04:10.513
Where different steps of that pipeline, the agent could say, like, hey, take a look at,

01:04:10.513 --> 01:04:14.032
like, the data frame here, or I want to pause here and, like, go before I go on to the next

01:04:14.032 --> 01:04:15.032
step.

01:04:15.032 --> 01:04:19.592
So for, for one extent, I think it's a really interesting substrate for human, like the

01:04:19.592 --> 01:04:23.312
notebook itself is a really interesting substrate for the humans and agents to sort of, like,

01:04:23.312 --> 01:04:24.312
express together.

01:04:24.312 --> 01:04:29.993
And the other is, I think, is really this idea of, like, you're sort of pushing the

01:04:29.993 --> 01:04:35.072
models thinking into, into, like, code and having this, like, artifact that comes out

01:04:35.072 --> 01:04:39.752
of that, like, session that you're working that resembles, like, what happened in that

01:04:39.752 --> 01:04:40.752
discussion.

01:04:40.752 --> 01:04:45.752
So, yeah, I was talking to the colleague about this recently, but, you know, if you, let's

01:04:45.752 --> 01:04:51.113
say I have, like, a problem at work and I'm working with some data and, you know, it's,

01:04:51.113 --> 01:04:55.273
I have, like, an instance of this problem where I open up my chat and I, like, start

01:04:55.273 --> 01:04:57.872
working on my, I don't have any extra tools.

01:04:57.872 --> 01:05:01.233
It's just the file system and maybe some Python and stuff.

01:05:01.233 --> 01:05:05.752
I might solve that instance of that problem with, like, a directory of some scripts and

01:05:05.752 --> 01:05:10.853
some outputs and a set of logs that describe, like, what that conversation was.

01:05:10.853 --> 01:05:14.192
But the next instance of that problem, the starting point is maybe, like, I point at

01:05:14.192 --> 01:05:19.112
that folder or I point at, like, and that conversation history from before.

01:05:19.112 --> 01:05:25.072
And by, like, sort of forcing some standardization of, like, what that reasoning was and that

01:05:25.072 --> 01:05:28.953
artifact that, like, came out of that session into something like a notebook, I think that

01:05:28.953 --> 01:05:34.192
can be a really powerful way to, like, bootstrap, like, solving instances of those problems

01:05:34.192 --> 01:05:38.713
in the future such that maybe you actually, you know, the next time you work on that type

01:05:38.713 --> 01:05:42.112
of data task, you start in a notebook and maybe you, you work really hard on a notebook

01:05:42.112 --> 01:05:46.513
that solves, like, a general version of that problem such that in the future, if you're

01:05:46.513 --> 01:05:51.112
picking up sessions and it's just editing a couple cells to, like, get back to, like,

01:05:51.112 --> 01:05:53.432
a very, like, similar type of output.

01:05:53.432 --> 01:05:59.953
So I think by having this, you know, like, artifact from, like, these interactive sessions

01:05:59.953 --> 01:06:04.432
with talking about data that do have the properties of code because it is code, like, that is

01:06:04.432 --> 01:06:09.672
a really interesting property of, like, yeah, doing data work that is, like, more reusable

01:06:09.672 --> 01:06:13.153
or sort of, like, a better starting point for some future work as well.

01:06:13.552 --> 01:06:18.713
I'm still trying to wrap my mind around Cloud Code just picking up Marimo on its own.

01:06:18.713 --> 01:06:25.393
Like, what do you, well, do you think there's a world where something like Cloud Code, even

01:06:25.393 --> 01:06:30.312
if the human didn't get involved, like that, that something like Cloud Code would benefit

01:06:30.312 --> 01:06:35.072
from Marimo, like, let's say I never read the output.

01:06:35.432 --> 01:06:42.953
Do you think that there's a world where it would benefit from Marimo in that?

01:06:44.153 --> 01:06:49.153
Yeah, well, so one thing is that these models, they've also been fine-tuned on, like, coding

01:06:49.153 --> 01:06:51.833
tasks on, like, using the file system.

01:06:51.833 --> 01:06:54.913
So they're really good at, like, grepping around and, like, looking for things.

01:06:54.913 --> 01:07:00.432
But, like, that can kind of pollute context or, like, you can kind of, like, get context

01:07:00.432 --> 01:07:06.112
very quickly if it needs to, like, scrub around to find, like, different values and things.

01:07:06.112 --> 01:07:10.112
So I have some hunch that, you know, there's been some work previously in, like, using

01:07:10.112 --> 01:07:14.552
Python REPLs with agents, but, like, you almost allow for the agent to put context

01:07:14.552 --> 01:07:18.432
in variables or in memory, but then they can, like, choose to query back with code such

01:07:18.432 --> 01:07:21.992
that, you know, instead of having to look at all the values of my data frame or skim

01:07:21.992 --> 01:07:26.593
through a CSV, maybe, like, I'll just load that as a thing in memory that then I can

01:07:26.593 --> 01:07:30.593
query multiple times more efficiently and get back, like, specific context.

01:07:30.593 --> 01:07:36.672
So and then I think, again, like, if it's sort of like, what is the output of that work?

01:07:36.672 --> 01:07:39.953
Maybe if it's just for, like, working on a data task where it's, like, one-off type

01:07:39.953 --> 01:07:44.793
of thing, just letting Cloud Code rip, like, with Python and the file system, I think is

01:07:44.793 --> 01:07:45.793
going to be totally fine.

01:07:45.793 --> 01:07:51.593
But, you know, if, you know, we do have users using Marimo pair that, like, are, like, deploying

01:07:51.593 --> 01:07:55.393
a lot of, like, headless versions of Marimo and that those all produce different Marimo

01:07:55.393 --> 01:07:56.393
notebooks.

01:07:56.393 --> 01:08:00.353
And then because it is that artifact, you can, like, spin up Marimo later and, like,

01:08:00.353 --> 01:08:05.232
look at what the agent produced and compare them or, like, you have this sort of substrate

01:08:05.232 --> 01:08:09.353
that then is more portable, or I can, you know, I don't have to necessarily give someone

01:08:09.353 --> 01:08:12.913
my chat history and, like, a bunch of files and, like, a PDF.

01:08:12.913 --> 01:08:17.713
I could, like, just give them the notebook that, like, the agent produced and pick up

01:08:17.713 --> 01:08:18.713
from there.

